Changes made
------------
1. Multiple enzymes
   digest_sequence() and get_concatemer_seqs() take a list of enzymes instead
   of one. Cut points are the sorted union of each enzyme's sites, de-duplicated
   (a position cut by two enzymes is one cut in the tube).

2. Version-independent site location  [src/pore_c_aqb/sites.py, new]
   Bio.Restriction.search() changed its answer at sequence boundaries between
   biopython 1.82 and 1.88, making the digest irreproducible across
   environments. Sites of palindromic enzymes are now located here under one
   documented rule. Non-palindromic (Type IIS) enzymes are still delegated to
   Biopython.

3. Enzyme validation  [src/pore_c_aqb/enzymes.py, new]
   All names are resolved and checked before any read is processed, with
   close-match suggestions on typos. Isoschizomers (DpnII/MboI) are collapsed.
   Enzymes that cut twice, or have no defined cut position, are rejected.

4. Per-enzyme site statistics  [DigestStats, src/pore_c_aqb/report.py, new]
   Reports how many recognition sites each enzyme has in the reads, its share
   of the cut points, and the observed site spacing beside the spacing expected
   by chance. Written to stderr and, with --stats, to TSV. The report states
   explicitly that a site count is not evidence an enzyme cut.

5. Enzyme table before the digest  [src/pore_c_aqb/report.py, new]
   Site, cut position drawn on both strands, sticky end and expected site
   spacing, shown before any data is read and on its own with --dry-run.

6. Junction motif analysis  [src/pore_c_aqb/junctions.py, new]
   Standalone command `pore-c-aqb-junctions`. Measures each enzyme's motif
   enrichment at ligation junctions in aligned monomers against a random
   background, which is the test of whether an enzyme actually cut. Not derived
   from upstream code.

7. Merging of falsely split fragments  [src/pore_c_aqb/merge.py, new]
   Standalone command `pore-c-aqb merge`. The in silico digest cuts at every
   recognition site, including the many that the enzyme did not cut in vitro,
   which inflates the Hi-C diagonal with contacts that never happened. This
   merges monomers that are consecutive along the read and contiguous on the
   genome, which can only be decided after alignment. Not derived from
   upstream code.

8. Upstream options implemented here
   --max_monomers, --excluded_list, --excluded_bam (exclusion of
   over-digested concatemers), --recursive and --glob (directory input), and
   both positional orders for INPUT and ENZYME. wf-pore-c passes these, and
   calls the digest with the enzyme first in one branch and the input file
   first in the other, so both are accepted.

   --max_monomers follows the semantics upstream introduced after 2.0.6: a
   concatemer cut into more pieces than the limit is dropped whole rather
   than trimmed.

9. Provenance
   A @PG line recording the tool, version, command line and enzyme list is
   added to the output BAM header.

10. Command-line interface  [src/pore_c_aqb/cli.py, new]
   Argument-compatible with `pore-c-py digest`; adds --stats and --dry-run.
